    "en": {
      "shared_perspectives": "German media, from liberal to conservative outlets, uniformly stress that the acute shortage of MINT‑qualified workers is a decisive bottleneck for the economy and that early talent pipelines and systematic reskilling programmes are now seen as essential policy priorities. The ongoing IG Metall tariff conflict is portrayed across the spectrum as a concrete illustration of the tension between wage growth and corporate competitiveness, leading to a shared call for reforms of collective‑bargaining structures and the financing of the social‑security system. At the same time, there is broad consensus that accelerating digitalisation, platform work and automation will fundamentally reshape employment, requiring coordinated measures to protect workers and to upgrade skills, even though the preferred policy instruments differ.",
